    "title": "Masters Par 3 Contest schedule, tee times and how to watch Wednesday's event",
    "text": "April 7, 2026, 2:14 p.m. ETBefore the 2026 Masters tournament begins on Thursday, April 9, there's another, smaller contest taking place at Augusta National Golf Club.\"Smaller\" is a key word in that it describes several aspects of the annual Par 3 Contest at the Masters. There's the smaller course – nine holes instead of the standard 18 for a round of golf. There's the smaller (lower) par, with every hole in the contest a par 3 hole. Then there are the participants, with golfers encouraged to invite their children and grandchildren to serve as caddies or take the occasional putt or chip shot.Last year, Colombian golfer Nico Echavarría won the contest. He went on to join a long line of Par 3 Contest winners when he failed to win the 2025 Masters, finishing in 51st.Here's what to know about the Par 3 Contest ahead of the 64th edition of the pre-Masters competition:How to watch 2026 Masters Par 3 ContestDate: Wednesday, April 8Where: Augusta National Golf Club in Augusta, GeorgiaTime: Noon ETTV: ESPN (2-4 p.m. ET)Streaming: Masters.com; Masters app; ESPN Unlimited & ESPN Select; Fubo (free trial)ESPN will broadcast the Masters Par 3 Contest on April 8. The first two hours of the competition will be streaming exclusive on ESPN's proprietary services and on the Masters app and Masters.com. Fans can also use cable alternative Fubo, which has a free trial, to stream ESPN's coverage of the Masters Par 3 Contest.Once ESPN's main coverage begins at 2 p.m. ET, former Philadelphia Eagles center Jason Kelce will join Scott Van Pelt and Marty Smith as one of ESPN's hosts.Stream the Par 3 Contest on FuboHow does the Par 3 Contest work?The Masters Par 3 Contest takes place on a nine-hole, par-27 golf course at Augusta National Golf Club. As with other rounds of golf, the winner is determined by which golfer has the lowest score in relation to par. Last year, Nico Echavarría won the contest when he finished his round 5 under then won a sudden-death playoff against J.J. Spaun.The first Masters Par 3 Contest was held in 1960. Since then, no golfer that has won the Par 3 Contest went on to win the Masters tournament.Masters Par 3 Contest tee timesTee times for the Par 3 Contest at the 2026 Masters have not yet been announced.This section will be updated.When is the 2026 Masters? Date, time, how to watchDates: Thursday, April 9 – Sunday, April 12Time: Honorary starters ceremony set for 7:30 a.m. ETFirst tee time 7:40 a.m. ET (John Keefer, Haotong Li)Where: August National Golf Club (Augusta, Georgia)TV: CBS, ESPNStreaming: Amazon Prime Video; Paramount+; ESPN Unlimited & ESPN Select; FuboThe 90th iteration of the Masters is scheduled to begin with its first round on Thursday, April 9. It will conclude with its fourth and final round on Sunday, April 12.There are many ways for fans to watch the Masters this year. Amazon Prime Video will offer early afternoon coverage of the first two rounds before ESPN takes over in the evenings. CBS provides primary broadcast coverage in the latter two rounds from 3-7 p.m. ET. Golf Channel fills in the broadcast gaps in the early mornings and late evenings.Cord-cutters can stream the Masters via the Masters app or Masters.com, with the proprietary streaming services of ESPN (ESPN Unlimited/Select) and CBS (Paramount+), or with cable alternative Fubo, which offers a free trial.Stream the 2026 Masters with Fubo",
